<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Slobodan Milošević</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1941">1941</a>–<a href="/years/2006">2006</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Slobodan Milošević was a Serbian politician who rose through the League of Communists to become president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> from 1989 and of the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> from 1997 to 2000. His Serbian nationalist policies contributed to the break-up of Yugoslavia and the wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. Ousted in 2000, he was handed to the Hague tribunal for war crimes and died in custody before a verdict.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/slobodan-milosevic.jpg" alt="Slobodan Milošević" width="320" height="442" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Stevan Kragujević · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stevan_Kragujevi%C4%87,_Slobodan_Milo%C5%A1evi%C4%87,_portret.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Milošević was born in Požarevac, four months after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/axis-powers">Axis</a> invasion of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>, and was raised during the occupation. His ancestral roots were in Montenegro. His father, a Serbian Orthodox theologian, died by suicide in 1962, and his mother, a teacher and active member of the Communist Party, in 1972. He studied law at the University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a>, where he befriended Ivan Stambolić, whose uncle Petar Stambolić had been president of the Serbian Executive Council. The connection proved crucial for his career, as the two climbed through the party hierarchy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">In office</h2><p>Milošević was elected president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgrade">Belgrade</a> city committee of the League of Communists in 1984. In 1987 he emerged as a force in Serbian politics by declaring support for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, who said the provincial government, dominated by ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a>, was oppressing them; he called for reducing Kosovo's autonomy. From 1988 the Anti-Bureaucratic Revolution, a series of rallies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vojvodina">Vojvodina</a> and Montenegro, led to the resignation of their governments and the election of officials allied with him. His supporters call it a genuine grass-roots movement.</p><p>He became president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> in 1989 and led the Socialist Party of Serbia from its foundation in 1990. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> sanctions during the Yugoslav Wars, Serbia's economy collapsed. In 1997 he became president of the Federal Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>. Counteraction against the Kosovo Liberation Army escalated into armed conflict in 1998 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> air strikes in 1999. After a disputed presidential election in 2000 he resigned amid demonstrations.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The International Criminal Tribunal for the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a> indicted him in May 1999 for crimes in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>, and later for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croatia">Croatia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocide</a>. Arrested in 2001 by Yugoslav authorities on suspicion of corruption, he was extradited to the tribunal, which he denounced as illegal; he refused counsel and conducted his own defence. He was found dead in his cell in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-hague">The Hague</a> on 11 March 2006, of a heart attack, before the trial concluded. Later verdicts found him part of a joint criminal enterprise that used violence to remove <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosniaks">Bosniaks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/albanians">Albanians</a> from parts of Croatia, Bosnia and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a>, and Kosovo. He remains a controversial figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Slobodan Milošević” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Real power lay with <b>Slobodan Milošević</b> and his Socialist Party of Serbia, who ran both republics as an effective dictatorship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1987 <b>Slobodan Milošević</b> came to power in Serbia and by populist means gained de facto control of Kosovo, Vojvodina and Montenegro, opposed by the leaders of Slovenia and Croatia.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Slobodan Milošević</b> had wished to make it Serbia's anthem, but the Socialist Party of Serbia chose &quot;March on the Drina&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/hey-slavs">Hey, Slavs</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Požarevac, in what is now <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scheveningen">Scheveningen</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/netherlands">the Netherlands</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.62,21.19&amp;year=1983" aria-label="Požarevac on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5056 586 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Slobodan Milošević"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5506 856h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5506 856h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.62,21.19&amp;year=1983">What was here in 1983? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
